# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# Environment — never commit real credentials; .env.example is the tracked template
.env
.env.local
!.env.example

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# OS
.DS_Store

# Agent tooling state. Gate files, verifier evidence, session handoffs and research notes
# are the scaffolding used to build this, not the thing being delivered. The whole
# directory is excluded rather than named subdirectories, because the tooling adds new
# state paths over time and a per-path list silently stops covering them.
.claude/

# Streamlit plants `.agents/skills/developing-with-streamlit` on server start, as a
# SYMLINK into `.venv`. It is generated, it is not ours, and `.venv` is excluded, so a
# committed copy would be a dangling link in every clone but the one that made it.
# Excluded at the top level for the same reason as `.claude/`: the set of paths a tool
# plants is not ours to enumerate.
.agents/

# Documentation is an allowlist, not a denylist. Everything under `docs/` is excluded and
# the deliverables are named back in below. A denylist was the first arrangement and it
# failed twice: a working note lands under a name no existing rule anticipated, sits
# untracked AND unignored, and one `git add -A` publishes it. An allowlist has no such
# state, because a new file is excluded until somebody decides otherwise.
docs/*
!docs/ADR/
!docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
!docs/DATA.md
!docs/EVAL.md
!docs/GUARDRAILS.md
!docs/CHARTS.md

# The rendered frames the documentation links to. The directory is re-included so its
# contents can be considered at all, then emptied again, then the four frames named: git
# cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so the order matters.
!docs/visuals/
docs/visuals/*
!docs/visuals/data.html
!docs/visuals/eval.html
!docs/visuals/guardrails.html
!docs/visuals/pipeline.html

# Reference material from an unrelated project, used only as a formatting example.
# It describes a different client's system and must never ship in this deliverable.
sample/

# Runtime artifacts
*.log
!eval/results/*.log

# eval/results/ IS tracked on purpose: the README cites these numbers, and the raw
# per-question output is the evidence for them. A reviewer should be able to check the
# claim rather than take it on trust.

# Generated reading material for the gold-set hand audit. Regenerated on demand by
# `python eval/audit.py worksheet`, so it carries nothing the repo does not already hold.
# The audit's OUTPUT, eval/gold_audit.yaml, is tracked: that is the evidence.
gold_audit_worksheet.md
